[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Frank James Marshall"]
[Result "1-0"]
[EventDate "1918.10.23"]
[ECO "C89"]
[WhiteElo "?"]
[BlackElo "?"]
[PlyCount "71"]
[Link "https://www.chess.com/blog/kahns/a-century-of-chess-new-york-1918"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3
d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 {In a somewhat mysterious game against Walter Frere, likely
played in 1917, Marshall played 9...e4.} 10. Nxe5 {\"I decided that I was in
honor bound, so to speak, to take the pawn and accept the challenge, as my
knowledge and judgment told me that my position should then be defensible,\"
Capablanca wrote.} 10... Nxe5 11. Rxe5 Nf6 {Later, of course, 11...c6 would
become the more common move.} 12. Re1 Bd6 13. h3 Ng4 {This line hasn't been
repeated much since this game. The point is that if 14.hxg4 Qh4 15.g3 Bxg3
16.fxg3 Qxg3+ 17.Kf1 Bxg4 wins, although a wild-and-crazy line has been deeply
analyzed involving 15.Qf3 Qh2+ 16.Kf1 Bxg4 17.Qxg4 Qh1+ 18.Ke2 Rae8+ when the
startling 19.Be6 $3 actually wins for white.} 14. Qf3 Qh4 15. d4 {15.Re8 leads to
nothing after 15...Bb7} 15... Nxf2 {The critical moment of the game. Black
retains pressure with 15...h5.} 16. Re2 {Likely, what Marshall had in mind was
16.Qxf2 when 16...Bg3 $4 loses to 17.Qxf7+ $1 but first 16...Bh2+ $1 17.Kf1 Bg3 would
allow black to retake with check after 18.Qxf7+} 16... Bg4 {After white's simple
reply, the knight is trapped, so Marshall keeps sacrificing.} 17. hxg4 Bh2+ 18.
Kf1 Bg3 {Every move here is complicated. Black could play 18...Nh1 $5 but after
19.Re3 Ng3+ 20.Ke1 white is ok.} 19. Rxf2 Qh1+ 20. Ke2 Bxf2 21. Bd2 Bh4 22. Qh3
Rae8+ 23. Kd3 {Capablanca is staying amazingly composed through a long,
difficult defense.} 23... Qf1+ 24. Kc2 Bf2 25. Qf3 Qg1 26. Bd5 c5 27. dxc5 Bxc5
28. b4 Bd6 29. a4 a5 30. axb5 axb4 31. Ra6 {Finally, on move 31, white develops
his queenside.} 31... bxc3 32. Nxc3 Bb4 33. b6 Bxc3 34. Bxc3 h6 35. b7 Re3 36.
Bxf7+ {Capablanca prevails but the win is far from pretty or smooth. It's fairly
clear, even from this game, that white's laggard queenside development gives
black plenty of compensation for the pawn.} 1-0